WHAT WAS TAKEN. Eight glyphs, held in one module.

`src/data/mapIcons.js` holds the artwork:

    aerialway        — the `aerial` transit class (téléphérique, télécabine),
                       drawn by `src/data/transitVehicleIcons.js`.

    harbor           — `military=naval_base`, drawn by
                       `src/data/militarySiteIcons.js`.

    bicycle          — the `bike` and `ebike` GBFS form factors,
    scooter          — the `moped` form factor (in France, always a Vespa-type
                       electric machine),
    car              — the `car` form factor (Citiz, Leo&Go, the municipal
                       carsharing networks),
    charging-station — the ELECTRIC badge that separates an e-bike from a pedal
                       bike,
                       all four drawn by `src/data/sharedMobilityIcons.js`.

    arrow            — the five commercial exchanges at France's frontiers,
                       drawn by `src/data/franceEnergy.js`.

    communications-tower
                     — the PANEL ROW icon of « Infrastructure numérique »,
                       declared in `src/data/layerTaxonomy.js` and masked by
                       `src/data/manager.js`. The only vendored glyph that is
                       not drawn on the globe.

`aerialway` replaced Material Symbols' `cable_car`, which is an interface glyph:
a boxed cabin on stubby legs that reads as a building at the 22 CSS px that
layer draws at. Maki authors for map labels in a 15-unit box, and its cabin
hangs from a cable, which is the one feature that says "aerial" rather than
"tram".

`bicycle`, `scooter` and `car` replaced Material Symbols' `pedal_bike`,
`electric_bike`, `electric_moped` and `directions_car` on 2026-09-14, on a
measurement rather than a preference. That layer draws at 7-20 CSS px, and at
those sizes Material's heavier frames close their own counters: the bicycle puts
86 px² of operator colour out of 88 into ONE contiguous patch at 17 px, so four
operators in the same Paris street read as the same blob in four hues. Maki's
bicycle keeps its counters open at 17 and still reads as a bicycle. No kick
scooter exists in either CC0 set — checked across the whole of Maki and all 557
Temaki icons on 2026-09-14 — so the trottinette keeps Material's
`electric_scooter`, and that is the only Material glyph left in that module.

`arrow` replaced a shape that module built out of Cesium primitives: a tube
swept along a great circle, terminated by a cone. The reader's verdict on it was
« moches », and the drawing agrees — an eight-sided `polylineVolume` with
`outline: true` draws its eight longitudinal edges, so 340 km of shaft read as
corrugated hose, and a cone is a triangle only from side-on. Maki's arrow also
arrives with a head 5.6 times the width of its shaft, which is the ratio that
module had spent a paragraph arguing for and could only reach by inventing a
factor of its own.

`communications-tower` replaced the character `▣` on 2026-09-14, and it is a
ROW icon rather than a map mark. That row carries three peers — data centres,
submarine cables and ANFR masts — and `▣` is the data centre module's own
square, so the row was named after one of its three members. A lattice mast
with radio waves is the sign for telecom infrastructure a reader already owns.

It is deliberately NOT on the map. `anfr-fr` draws its supports at 4,5 to 11
CSS px (18 px when one is selected), and a bare silhouette on orthophoto stops
being findable under about 18 px — measured on three real crops for the
military-sites layer. The masts keep their dots; the glyph lives at the 16 px
the panel gives it, painted through a CSS mask so it takes the row's own colour
and dims with it.

`harbor` replaced Material Symbols' `directions_boat`, a passenger ferry seen
head-on. The OSM tag says arsenal, not boat, and the ferry's superstructure did
not survive being punched into a 16 px plate. An anchor is the sign every
nautical chart already uses and it is a single compact mass.

WHAT WAS CHANGED. No coordinate.

Only the `d` path string is vendored, in Maki's own `0 0 15 15` space, NOT
rescaled — rescaling is a redraw, and a redraw is no longer the artwork that was
evaluated.

One mechanical normalisation, which changes no number: Maki's published SVG
breaks long `d` attributes with XML character escapes (`&#xA;&#x9;`, a newline
plus a tab). Those are whitespace to an SVG path parser and are resolved to
single spaces so the string can live in JavaScript source. Every command letter,
every coordinate and their order are untouched, and
`src/data/mapIcons.test.mjs` pins them.

At render time the path is drawn TWICE by `mapIcons.js`: once as a wide dark
stroke (a halo, so the glyph stays readable over pale terrain) and once as a
white fill (so Cesium's per-billboard colour multiply can tint it). The viewBox
is padded to `-1 -1 17 17` so that halo is not clipped by the canvas edge. The
path geometry itself is untouched.

`sharedMobilityIcons.js` composes them differently and no more invasively: the
path is PUNCHED out of a tinted plate through an SVG `<mask>`, and the transform
that fits a 15-unit box into that 96-unit plate is a transform, not a rewrite —
the coordinates reach the SVG renderer exactly as vendored, which is what
`src/data/sharedMobilityIcons.test.mjs` asserts by looking for the path string
itself in what the module draws.

`franceEnergy.js` composes `arrow` a third way, and it is still no rewrite. It
draws three passes over the one path — a wide dark halo, then a translucent
body and a near-opaque edge, the two alphas taken from the prisms that share its
map — and it wraps them in a `rotate(-90 7.5 7.5)` so the arrow points UP,
which is the contract `src/data/iconOrientation.js` expects of any glyph it aims.
The billboard then scales that square texture into a rectangle, stretching the
glyph along its own axis by the megawatts it stands for. A rotation and a scale
applied to the QUAD and to the canvas move no coordinate: the texture is
rasterised from the unmodified path, and the string in this project's source is
Maki's own.
